A patient reactivation campaign is a message to the patients who finished care and were never invited back. They already know you and already trust you. For most practices open more than a few years, it is the fastest way to fill gaps in the schedule, and it costs nothing in ads.

One of our clients, a chiropractic clinic in Tampa, had eleven years of patients in the practice software and nobody contacted after their last visit. We wrote to 640 of them. 31 rebooked in the first month. The full case is on our [results page](/results/), and the steps below are the ones we followed.

## Who to write to

Open your practice software and pull every patient with a last visit twelve months ago or more. Then take out three groups:

- anyone who told you they were leaving, moving or unhappy
- anyone who asked you not to contact them
- anyone whose records show they should not be invited back, for example a patient who was discharged to another provider

What is left is your list. It is usually longer than you expect. The Tampa clinic had been open eleven years, and the list came to 640 people.

Do not trim it to the patients you remember. The ones you do not remember are the point.

## What to write

Write the message as the doctor, not as the practice. People came to see a person, and that person asking them back is what works. Three rules keep it honest and short:

1. **One reason to come now.** "It has been a while since we checked your spine" is enough for a chiropractic patient. A dental patient gets "you are due a check-up". Do not list every service you offer.
1. **One way to reply.** A booking link, or "reply to this email and we will find you a time". Not both, and not a phone number that goes to voicemail at lunch.
1. **No discount.** Several state chiropractic boards have rules on advertised free or discounted services, and for Medicare and Medicaid patients a free service can count as an improper inducement under federal law. A plain invitation does not need one.

Keep it to four or five sentences. Nothing about the patient's diagnosis or treatment goes in the message, because an email can be read by someone else in the house.

Here is the shape of the message we sent for the Tampa clinic, with the wording changed:

> Hi Maria, it has been over a year since your last visit, and I wanted to check in. If your back has been fine, I am glad. If it has not, we have times this week and next. Reply to this email and Jenna will find you one. Dr. Carter

That is the whole thing. No banner, no offer, no list of services.

## How to send it

Email, in batches, over two or three weeks. Sending 640 messages in one morning means 640 replies in one morning, and the front desk cannot answer them all the same day. Fifty to a hundred a day is a pace a small team can keep up with.

Send from the doctor's own address or one that a reply reaches. A message from "noreply" asks people to book and then refuses to hear from them.

Two weeks after the first message, send one more to the people who did not open or reply. Then stop. A third message starts to feel like a chase.

## Answer every reply the same day

This is where campaigns fail. A patient who replies "yes, can I come Thursday?" and hears nothing for two days has usually booked somewhere else, or decided it was not that important.

Decide before you send who answers, and when. For the Tampa clinic, every reply got an answer the same day, with a time offered in the first answer. The reply rate is what the campaign is worth, and the answer speed is what turns a reply into a visit. We go into this on our [patient reactivation](/services/patient-reactivation/) page.

## Ask for a review after the visit

A patient who came back after a year and had a good visit is the easiest person to ask for a review. Ask on the day, with a link that opens the review box directly, and ask everyone, not just the ones you think will say yes.

For the Tampa clinic we added a review request after every completed visit, and the same campaign that rebooked 31 patients also brought 19 new Google reviews. The figures are on our [results page](/results/). If you want the rules on asking for and answering reviews, our [Google Business Profile guide](/blog/google-business-profile-for-dentists/) covers them, and they apply to any practice.

## What one Tampa clinic got

The whole campaign, in the numbers as we published them on our [results page](/results/):

- 640 patients contacted, all not seen in 12 months or more
- 31 rebooked in month one
- 19 new Google reviews
- zero ad spend

In the owner's words: "Patients we assumed had moved away. They just hadn't been asked."

That is one clinic, one campaign, and the first month. Your list, your specialty and how long people have been away all change the result. What does not change is that the list already exists in your software and nobody has used it.

## What a reactivation campaign will not do

It will not fix a practice that patients left for a reason. If the last visits were rushed or the front desk was short with people, the message brings a few back and the rest stay away. Fix the visit first.

It will not replace new patients. Reactivation refills the schedule from people you already have. Growing past that still needs people who have never heard of you, which is what [ads](/services/paid-ads/) and a good profile are for. Our [chiropractic marketing](/industries/chiropractic/) page sets out the order we do these in.

And it will not run itself after the first month. The list grows again every month as patients finish care. The practices that keep the schedule full set a recall that goes out when each patient is due, so a reactivation campaign is something they only need once.
